OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/440#issuecomment-220642998"},"message":"No, you got it backwards. There is always a HOST. On Linux it also needs three slashes, if there is no HOST.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1463759432,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMDY0MzkyNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/440#issuecomment-220643926"},"message":"Yes, if you remove HOST from your pattern, then there are always three\nslashes. The differnece is, that on linux an absolute path already starts\nwith a slash. On windows it doesn't. So the difference is not, what the URL\nlooks like, but how to build it from what you have.\n\n2016-05-20 17:47 GMT+02:00 Michael Hanke notifications@github.com:\n\n\u003e No, you got it backwards. There is always a HOST. On Linux it also needs\n\u003e three slashes, if there is no HOST.\n\u003e \n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you commented.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly or view it on GitHub\n\u003e https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/440#issuecomment-220642998","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1463759824,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMDY0NTY4Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/440#issuecomment-220645683"},"message":"Well, the function is broken. It yields invalid URLs on Linux, unless I make sure the path is absolute. That was the sole point.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1463759859,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMDY0NTgyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/440#issuecomment-220645824"},"message":"And in order to avoid this, we should use urlparse to build URLs.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464711549,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMjc0MDczNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-222740734"},"message":"YES.  And it is simply wrong since for some obnoxious (it wasn't late but was Friday!) reason I thought that `#` is used to separate out options in the url (should be `?`).  So should be fixed, may be RF'ed to use urlparse, and unittests provided","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464728526,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMjgxOTQzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-222819434"},"message":"Maan... I am really not sure what bright idea I had there to use '#' instead of '?' in the urls, but I did use it in construction of urls pointing to data within archives to embed information about the size of the file, e.g. currently for openfmri we have \n\n```\ndl+archive:MD5E-s2527262329--bd3ea399057c529b37b09dcecec1ca60.0raw.tgz/ds001_R1.1.0/sub001/BOLD/task001_run001/bold.nii.gz#size=47241449\n```\n\nas an example \"url\" to point to the content within file, while giving us also information about the file size.  I don't see any conscious note from myself to ourselves on why I have chosen `#` over `?`, so may be I indeed just mixed up at that point, or may be I wanted to use explicitly the portion after '#' which is not processed by webservers (unlike url options after `?` being passed into the request) to provide a supplementary information which we could use.  Usually `#id` is used within urls to point webbrowsers to specific id within the page, so I don't think there is any RFC/standard regulating what should or should not be in there.\nSince we do not have any really publicly consumed annexes yet, we could RF (as @glalteva did in #491) to use \"correct\" `?` but I wonder if we should do it; or just keep `#` for this specific case (internal supplementary information) ?  @hanke -- I will let you decide ;) and we will do it (I might still provide compatibility for now so we don't need to recrawl all test datasets for now).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464791263,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzAwOTY5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223009698"},"message":"ok -- read more on it. \nIn general there is no ultimate standard of what should or should not be in the fragment and various RFCs etc describe different usages for `#fragment` for different mime types, see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fragment_identifier .\nIn the original RFC (https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3986) the \"#fragment\" is described as\n\n   The fragment identifier component of a URI allows indirect\n   identification of a secondary resource by reference to a primary\n   resource and additional identifying information.  The identified\n   secondary resource may be some portion or subset of the primary\n   resource, some view on representations of the primary resource, or\n   some other resource defined or described by those representations.\n\nSo theoretically this could be the most conformant way to actually point to the file within the archive.  It also sounds legit for specifying the size (\"additional identifying information\") or checksums (I did see emails using fragment specifying e.g. checksum like `#md5=xxxxxx`, which is what lead me to this use of `#` here).  So ideally I guess instead of \n`dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz/1/d2/2d#size=123`\nwe could have used\n`dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d;size=123`\nThis way we could theoretically even generate/support urls like `http://example.com/bla.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d;size=123`.\n\nI think I will take over #491 from @glalteva , RF it for use of `#path=` while providing compatibility with 'old' more custom specification format so we don't need to regenerate all those annexes for now.  But eventually we might regenerate them anyways so they all would get more 'compliant' urls.\n\n@hanke what do you think?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1464793747,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzAyMzQ0NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223023445"},"message":"Sounds reasonable to me.\nNevertheless, I think one can see it two ways. It's either specifying a\nsecondary ressource within the archive or it's a query to the special\nremote. I'd still prefer the latter, but it's a matter of taste, I guess.\n\n2016-06-01 16:27 GMT+02:00 Yaroslav Halchenko notifications@github.com:\n\n\u003e ok -- read more on it.\n\u003e In general there is no ultimate standard of what should or should not be\n\u003e in the fragment and various RFCs etc describe different usages for\n\u003e #fragment for different mime types, see\n\u003e https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fragment_identifier .\n\u003e In the original RFC (https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3986) the \"#fragment\"\n\u003e is described as\n\u003e \n\u003e The fragment identifier component of a URI allows indirect\n\u003e identification of a secondary resource by reference to a primary\n\u003e resource and additional identifying information. The identified\n\u003e secondary resource may be some portion or subset of the primary\n\u003e resource, some view on representations of the primary resource, or\n\u003e some other resource defined or described by those representations.\n\u003e \n\u003e So theoretically this could be the most conformant way to actually point\n\u003e to the file within the archive. It also sounds legit for specifying the\n\u003e size (\"additional identifying information\") or checksums (I did see emails\n\u003e using fragment specifying e.g. checksum like #md5=xxxxxx, which is what\n\u003e lead me to this use of # here). So ideally I guess instead of\n\u003e dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz/1/d2/2d#size=123\n\u003e we could have used\n\u003e dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d;size=123\n\u003e This way we could theoretically even generate/support urls like\n\u003e http://example.com/bla.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d;size=123.\n\u003e \n\u003e I think I will take over #491\n\u003e https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/491 from @glalteva\n\u003e https://github.com/glalteva , RF it for use of #path= while providing\n\u003e compatibility with 'old' more custom specification format so we don't need\n\u003e to regenerate all those annexes for now. But eventually we might regenerate\n\u003e them anyways so they all would get more 'compliant' urls.\n\u003e \n\u003e @hanke https://github.com/hanke what do you think?\n\u003e \n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you are subscribed to this thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223009698,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e https://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe/AKAw_Wnu8Ht2-657iJerUkf28d_h6Gfwks5qHZbggaJpZM4IjUKQ\n\u003e .","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464794033,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzAyNDk5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223024998"},"message":"\u003e    Sounds reasonable to me.\n\u003e    Nevertheless, I think one can see it two ways. It's either specifying a\n\u003e    secondary ressource within the archive or it's a query to the special\n\u003e    remote. I'd still prefer the latter, but it's a matter of taste, I guess.\n\nindeed... especially given that the special remote is the one which\nprovides that piece... but size is nohow used by it really ATM -- I\nadded it so later we could get a sense of size even without relying on\nparsing annex key which we might not have at the point of url\nhandling. I guess remote could use it as well to verify the size of\nextracted file.\neh heh -- now I am again undecided ;)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464891476,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzM3NjkwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223376906"},"message":"@joeyh please share your wisdom!  Which of the 3 url \"schemes\" you would prefer to refer to the content coming from an archive\n1. (current) `dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz/1/d2/2d#size=123`\n   - pros: objectively none, but that is what I have already in few annexes\n   - cons: somewhat adhoc which wouldn't be understood by anyone else\n2. (server options) `dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz?path=1/d2/2d\u0026size=123`\n   - pros: more inline with the fact that extraction atm happens within special remote (alike on server)\n   - cons: `size` is not really anything for server to worry about besides may be for checking, but then may be  it could as well become `#size=123` (see 3)\n3. (fragment) `dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d\u0026size=123`\n   - pros: somewhat inline with the idea of a `fragment`, we can theoretically use it for some regular public urls (e.g. `http://example.com/bla.tar.gz#path=...`) and theoretically other \"clients\" could support understanding of the 'path' and 'size' arguments here for mime types corresponding to archives\n   - cons: it is not really handled by 'annex' as a client, but I guess could be paraphrased that special remote is the client which annex delegates the processing\n\nor neither of those? ;-)\n\nedit:  should use `\u0026` not `;` as a separator","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1464893257,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzM4NTU2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223385560"},"message":"As far as git-annex is concerned, these should all be fine, since it\njust passes the url along and does not try to parse it. (And if it did,\nit would treat urls differing only in #fragment as different.)\n\nI sort of like your thinking in #3.\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464909926,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzQ1MTg5MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223451891"},"message":"so let it be #3 upon @joeyh 's blessing ;)  will do that, while still supporting 1. style urls for compatibility at least for now (they might need to go later when I would need to analyze/extend available per key urls)","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1465496018,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50Njg3NjE4NTg5"},"status":2}]}